[{:db/id "q-177648/selection/f4cb7e4339c90604025175ddab67ce306145420e7ebc6d4f3ed2ba2a7b61a256" :answer/id #uuid "224e3ace-a61a-5d41-a786-914f70e37870" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/ec0e1df36bf5d93b8cdbcc3d14cd510fbe299b929ed3d19f4d901f4461093305.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177648/selection/3c51eb96c3a635fc794879c35e9018b6b348e7aa78528f39a20feb67a43e14de" :answer/id #uuid "16200592-2582-5e08-afd0-fd38b5bd26e0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/8627676ae3b7bcdeff423d79d7289d1d3f9243e0cc69d4f3ac7d3e5c51141fc1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177648/selection/bc5bf24b066f0f3156d4435e9245e2cf2575546b3d03199835d9208545b7d295" :answer/id #uuid "eb992b3b-93b4-5214-9df1-76d829603537" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/c87871c50b343792c73fc18dfe92bc2065b18ed3de1e7bb70324b8b43d027206.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177648/selection/f27434110d4070ecd4dd7fc288480ebbd83cb1e657b0b380a8e23a534f2b4776" :answer/id #uuid "aeeea064-15fc-5148-b5dd-7b7202d68499" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/39db7bc1b259b222a2365441babc3421101961ea26a7143af14b526ba7f81200.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177648/selection/799751b33f74062136b89ae26561c37fd4d650f5e225cab2b1eccab245807df2" :answer/id #uuid "3948bd00-4939-5683-ba62-adc9eea341a1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/f90de7c6d88b187af3604912a647620fb88d4833e08fd086cf1a9daae52f331f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-177648/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c549a83-1b44-52cf-81d1-2272082f24bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-177648/selection/f27434110d4070ecd4dd7fc288480ebbd83cb1e657b0b380a8e23a534f2b4776" :answer-field/choices ["q-177648/selection/f4cb7e4339c90604025175ddab67ce306145420e7ebc6d4f3ed2ba2a7b61a256" "q-177648/selection/3c51eb96c3a635fc794879c35e9018b6b348e7aa78528f39a20feb67a43e14de" "q-177648/selection/bc5bf24b066f0f3156d4435e9245e2cf2575546b3d03199835d9208545b7d295" "q-177648/selection/f27434110d4070ecd4dd7fc288480ebbd83cb1e657b0b380a8e23a534f2b4776" "q-177648/selection/799751b33f74062136b89ae26561c37fd4d650f5e225cab2b1eccab245807df2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-177648" :question/id #uuid "60baa69f-bbf2-5905-831d-ab5e879fa2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-177648" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,1].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,0.5]\\times [0,1]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,0.5],b\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0.5,0),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/5b882f3fb0e7b44eb2df3a510260574e5c4a4d85842a9c0d65515f1a40894756.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,i]\\times [0,1]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,i],b\\in [0,1]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(i,0),$ $(i,1),$ and $(0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/cb530698591dbd71a8058d9faa0c60b636dc654f21e92d65ec8295aeaa67355a.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment ${0}\\times [0,1]:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/704f8f6f0666d09bb2b8a25eab0548c3eb808e2c9d394d2ae873de35cb0112d1.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-177648/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-177648"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-177649"] :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-1,1]\\times [-0.5,0.5]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-0.5),$ $(1,0.5),$ $(-1,0.5),$ and $(-1,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/f0a530434b8ac38fdb4cb517f22e121892c8b7c635c702c1cd9fccb2961aee18.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-1,1]\\times [-i,i]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(1,-i),$ $(1,i),$ $(-1,i),$ and $(-1,-i).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/6982658bd8ce0e3c81953309ed9f2a70d1e56bbfce8af8862a32da66bf19e638.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[-1,1]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/34a3addbdcf3d5348805f494ed2e427f21f6ad424b0d9d2a411dae9f99b2fb7a.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181303"] :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181979"] :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-184504"] :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times {0.5}={(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times {1-i}={(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
